Summary

  While investigating repeated development-server memory restarts in a Next.js 16.2.9 + React 19.2.x app, I
  traced the retained memory to React Flight's development-only async-debug tracking in
  react-server-dom-turbopack-server.

  Heap snapshots show a bounded number of live pendingOperations entries retaining a much larger, growing
  ancestry graph through .previous / .awaited / .promise (WeakRef) links. A long-lived async context (e.g. an
  open stream / keepalive doing periodic awaited work) keeps the tail of an ancestry chain alive, which
  transitively pins the entire lineage. The retained graph grows without bound for the lifetime of the context.

  This does not occur in production builds (the tracking is *.development.js-only).

  I want to keep the claim narrow: React's dev async-ancestry tracking appears to retain unbounded lineage under
  long-lived async workloads — not "React has a memory leak."

  Environment

  - react-server-dom-turbopack 19.2.7 (bundled by Next.js 16.2.9), and reproduces on latest canary
  19.3.0-canary-e92ecef2-20260618 (the four async-hook callbacks are byte-for-byte identical between the two).
  - Node v22.21.1
  - Development mode only (NODE_ENV !== 'production')
  - The tracking is installed unconditionally via async_hooks.createHook({...}).enable() in
  react-server-dom-turbopack-server.node.development.js.

  Observed behavior

  A long-lived async workload causes retained ancestry to grow continuously even though the number of live
  operations stays bounded.

  From one captured in-flight heap snapshot of the dev server:
  - pendingOperations Map: ~20,377 live entries
  - Retained ancestry reachable from it: ~1.5M nodes (~1.25M objects via .awaited, ~842K via .previous, ~1.5M
  WeakRef)
  - Path-to-root of a sampled WeakRef:
  WeakRef ←promise← Object ←previous← Object ←previous← … (deep .previous chain) …
          ←awaited← Object ←[element]← (array) ←table← Map ←pendingOperations← <module/global> → (GC root)

  Cardinality finding (the key signal): the entry count is bounded (~20K) while the ancestry graph is unbounded
  (~1.5M). This points to ancestry accumulation, not failed eviction — destroy is removing entries, but a live
  entry's .previous chain retains its whole ancestry.

  Expected behavior

  Development-time async-debug metadata should not retain unbounded ancestry for long-lived async workloads
  (e.g. via a depth/size bound or pruning of old lineage), so a single long-lived async context cannot pin an
  ever-growing .previous chain.

  Standalone reproduction

  This copies the four async_hooks callbacks verbatim from react-server-dom-turbopack-server.node.development.js
  (helpers that don't affect retention are stubbed) and drives them with a continuous-await loop standing in
  for a long-lived stream. No React, no Next, no app code. Run with node --expose-gc repro.mjs.

  import async_hooks from 'node:async_hooks';
  import { performance } from 'node:perf_hooks';

  // --- React Flight dev callbacks, copied verbatim from
  // react-server-dom-turbopack-server.node.development.js (19.2.7 == canary 19.3.0).
  // Only React-internal helpers are stubbed; node shapes + previous/awaited linking are unchanged.
  const pendingOperations = new Map();
  let lastRanAwait = null;
  const emptyStack = [];
  const resolveOwner = () => null;
  const resolveRequest = () => null;
  const parseStackTracePrivate = () => null;
  function resolvePromiseOrAwaitNode(n, end) { n.tag = n.tag === 3 ? 1 : 2; n.end = end; return n; }

  async_hooks.createHook({
    init(asyncId, type, triggerAsyncId, resource) {
      var trigger = pendingOperations.get(triggerAsyncId);
      if ("PROMISE" === type)
        if (((type = async_hooks.executionAsyncId()), type !== triggerAsyncId)) {
          if (void 0 === trigger) return;
          triggerAsyncId = null;
          if (null === trigger.stack || (2 !== trigger.tag && 4 !== trigger.tag)) {
            resource = new WeakRef(resource);
            var request = resolveRequest(); null !== request && (triggerAsyncId = null);
          } else (triggerAsyncId = emptyStack),
            (resource = void 0 !== resource._debugInfo ? new WeakRef(resource) : trigger.promise);
          type = pendingOperations.get(type);
          trigger = { tag: 4, owner: resolveOwner(), stack: triggerAsyncId, start: performance.now(), end: -1.1,
  promise: resource, awaited: trigger, previous: void 0 === type ? null : type };
        } else (type = resolveOwner()),
          (trigger = { tag: 3, owner: type, stack: null === type ? null : parseStackTracePrivate(), start:
  performance.now(), end: -1.1, promise: new WeakRef(resource), awaited: void 0 === trigger ? null : trigger,
  previous: null });
      else if ("bound-anonymous-fn" === type || "Microtask" === type || "TickObject" === type || "Immediate" ===
  type) { if (void 0 === trigger) return; }
      else if (void 0 === trigger)
        (trigger = resolveOwner()), (trigger = { tag: 0, owner: trigger, stack: null === trigger ?
  parseStackTracePrivate() : null, start: performance.now(), end: -1.1, promise: null, awaited: null, previous:
  null });
      else if (2 === trigger.tag || 4 === trigger.tag)
        (resource = resolveOwner()), (trigger = { tag: 0, owner: resource, stack: null === resource ?
  parseStackTracePrivate() : null, start: performance.now(), end: -1.1, promise: null, awaited: null, previous:
  trigger });
      pendingOperations.set(asyncId, trigger);
    },
    before(asyncId) {
      var node = pendingOperations.get(asyncId);
      if (void 0 !== node) switch (node.tag) {
        case 0: lastRanAwait = null; 0 > node.end ? (node.end = performance.now())
          : ((node = { tag: 0, owner: node.owner, stack: node.stack, start: node.start, end: performance.now(),
  promise: node.promise, awaited: node.awaited, previous: node.previous }), pendingOperations.set(asyncId,
  node)); break;
        case 4: lastRanAwait = resolvePromiseOrAwaitNode(node, performance.now()); break;
        case 2: lastRanAwait = node; break;
        case 3: resolvePromiseOrAwaitNode(node, performance.now()).previous = lastRanAwait; lastRanAwait = null;
  break;
        default: lastRanAwait = null;
      }
    },
    promiseResolve(asyncId) {
      var node = pendingOperations.get(asyncId);
      if (void 0 !== node) {
        switch (node.tag) { case 4: case 3: node = resolvePromiseOrAwaitNode(node, performance.now()); break;
  case 2: case 1: break; default: throw Error("IO_NODE bug"); }
        var currentAsyncId = async_hooks.executionAsyncId();
        asyncId !== currentAsyncId && ((asyncId = pendingOperations.get(currentAsyncId)),
          1 === node.tag ? (node.awaited = void 0 === asyncId ? null : asyncId)
            : void 0 !== asyncId && ((currentAsyncId = { tag: 2, owner: node.owner, stack: node.stack, start:
  node.start, end: node.end, promise: node.promise, awaited: node.awaited, previous: node.previous }),
              (node.start = node.end), (node.end = performance.now()), (node.previous = currentAsyncId),
  (node.awaited = asyncId)));
      }
    },
    destroy(asyncId) { pendingOperations.delete(asyncId); },
  }).enable();

  // --- measure retained ancestry reachable from live Map entries (after a full GC) ---
  function measure() {
    global.gc();
    const seen = new Set(); let maxDepth = 0;
    for (const node of pendingOperations.values()) {
      let depth = 0, n = node;
      while (n && typeof n === 'object' && !seen.has(n)) { seen.add(n); depth++; n = n.previous || n.awaited; }
      if (depth > maxDepth) maxDepth = depth;
    }
    return { mapSize: pendingOperations.size, retainedAncestryNodes: seen.size, maxChainDepth: maxDepth, rssMB:
  Math.round(process.memoryUsage().rss / 1048576) };
  }

  // --- long-lived async context doing periodic awaited work (stands in for a stream) ---
  let running = true;
  (async function longLivedStream() {
    while (running) { await new Promise((r) => setImmediate(r)); await Promise.resolve(); await
  Promise.resolve(); await Promise.resolve(); }
  })();

  let tick = 0;
  const iv = setInterval(() => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({ second: ++tick, ...measure() }));
    if (tick >= 8) { running = false; clearInterval(iv); }
  }, 1000);

  Output (Node v22.21.1; same logic as canary 19.3.0-canary-e92ecef2-…):
  {"second":1,"mapSize":1397,"retainedAncestryNodes":132464,"maxChainDepth":131070,"rssMB":169}
  {"second":3,"mapSize":392992,"retainedAncestryNodes":711602,"maxChainDepth":318614,"rssMB":412}
  {"second":5,"mapSize":382248,"retainedAncestryNodes":1090498,"maxChainDepth":708254,"rssMB":556}
  {"second":8,"mapSize":340288,"retainedAncestryNodes":1585710,"maxChainDepth":1245426,"rssMB":699}

  mapSize plateaus (~340–390K) while retainedAncestryNodes grows linearly and a single .previous chain reaches
  ~1.24M deep. With the default old-space size, this OOMs a bare Node process in ~36 s.

  Note on async_hooks destroy (ruling out the obvious explanation)

  A separate async_hooks probe on Node v22.21.1 showed destroy firing for 800,009 of 800,013 promise resources
  (≈100%), so this is not "destroy never fires." Eviction works; the retention is via .previous/.awaited strong
  references pinned by a long-lived live entry, not stuck Map entries.

  { "node": "v22.21.1", "promiseInit": 800013, "promiseDestroy": 800009, "destroyRate": "1.0000" }

  Additional artifacts (available on request)

  - The in-flight .heapsnapshot and the retainer/path-to-root and pendingOperations-cardinality analyses. (Not
  attaching the multi-hundred-MB snapshot up front.)
